## 8. Collections Framework

```
Iterable → Collection ─┬─ List  (ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, Stack)
                       ├─ Set   (HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet)
                       └─ Queue (PriorityQueue, ArrayDeque, LinkedList)
Map (HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap, Hashtable, ConcurrentHashMap)  [separate hierarchy]
```

### List
```java
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("A"); list.add("B"); list.add(1, "X");
list.get(0); list.remove("B"); list.size(); list.contains("A");
Collections.sort(list);
List<Integer> imm = List.of(1, 2, 3);   // immutable
```
| ArrayList | LinkedList |
|-----------|------------|
| Dynamic array | Doubly linked list |
| Fast random access O(1) | Fast insert/delete at ends O(1) |
| Slow middle insert O(n) | Slow get O(n) |

### Set
```java
Set<Integer> hs = new HashSet<>();      // unordered, unique
Set<Integer> lhs = new LinkedHashSet<>();// insertion order
Set<Integer> ts = new TreeSet<>();      // sorted
hs.add(1); hs.add(1);                   // size stays 1
```

### Map
```java
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("a", 1);
map.putIfAbsent("b", 2);
map.get("a");
map.getOrDefault("z", 0);
map.containsKey("a");
map.merge("a", 1, Integer::sum);        // word-count style

for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> e : map.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println(e.getKey() + "=" + e.getValue());
}
```

**How HashMap works:** key → `hashCode()` → bucket index → entries stored in a linked list (converted to a red-black tree when a bucket exceeds 8 entries, Java 8+). `equals()` resolves collisions. Default capacity 16, load factor 0.75, doubles on resize.

### Queue / Deque
```java
Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
q.offer(1); q.poll(); q.peek();

PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();            // min-heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxPq = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();
dq.push(1); dq.pop();   // use as stack
```

### Iterator
```java
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next().equals("A")) it.remove();   // safe removal
}
```

### Comparable vs Comparator
```java
class Emp implements Comparable<Emp> {
    String name; int sal;
    public int compareTo(Emp o) { return Integer.compare(sal, o.sal); } // natural order
}

list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Emp::getName).thenComparing(Emp::getSal));
list.sort(Comparator.comparingInt((Emp e) -> e.sal).reversed());
```

